 ],
 "sentences": [
  {
   "id": "co-maya.1",
   "scene": "co-maya",
   "text": "Maya locked in 7.62%.",
   "start": 0.9,
   "end": 3.94
  },
  {
   "id": "co-today.1",
   "scene": "co-today",
   "text": "This week's average: 7.03%.",
   "start": 4.24,
   "end": 7.38
  },
  {
   "id": "co-cost.1",
   "scene": "co-cost",
   "text": "Refinancing costs $5,124.",
   "start": 7.68,
   "end": 11.06
  },
  {
   "id": "co-question.1",
   "scene": "co-question",
   "text": "So when does that money come back?",
   "start": 11.36,
   "end": 14.72
  },
  {
   "id": "a1-lab.1",
   "scene": "a1-lab",
   "text": "This is a lab for money decisions: we run the numbers on public data, and show every assumption.",
   "start": 17.72,
   "end": 23.86
  },
  {
   "id": "a1-scope.1",
   "scene": "a1-scope",
   "text": "US only. The rates here are history, not a forecast.",
   "start": 24.31,
   "end": 28.16
  },
  {
   "id": "a1-rehook.1",
   "scene": "a1-rehook",
   "text": "By the end, you will see the exact rate drop that pays back a refinance like Maya's within 36 months, and how every rate drop since 1971 actually played out.",
   "start": 30.3,
   "end": 42.69
  },
  {
   "id": "a1-maya.1",
   "scene": "a1-maya",
   "text": "She borrowed $375,000 in October 2023, the month the 30-year rate peaked.",
   "start": 43.14,
   "end": 50.49
  },
  {
   "id": "a1-you.1",
   "scene": "a1-you",
   "text": "And if your own rate starts with a 7, the question is yours as well.",
   "start": 50.94,
   "end": 56.29
  },
  {
   "id": "a1-q.1",
   "scene": "a1-q",
   "text": "First: is Maya's bill unusual?",
   "start": 56.74,
   "end": 59.29
  },
  {
   "id": "a1-median.1",
   "scene": "a1-median",
   "text": "No. In 2025, the median closing cost on a rate-and-term refinance was $5,124.",
   "start": 59.74,
   "end": 68.54
  },
  {
   "id": "a1-hmda.1",
   "scene": "a1-hmda",
   "text": "Lenders must report every mortgage they make, and since 2018, the total loan costs of each one.",
   "start": 70.04,
   "end": 77.55
  },
  {
   "id": "a1-count.1",
   "scene": "a1-count",
   "text": "That is the middle of 488,241 such loans that lenders reported under the Home Mortgage Disclosure Act.",
   "start": 78.0,
   "end": 86.96
  },
  {
   "id": "a1-spread.1",
   "scene": "a1-spread",
   "text": "Half of those borrowers paid between $3,443 and $8,270.",
   "start": 87.41,
   "end": 95.55
  },
  {
   "id": "a1-terms.1",
   "scene": "a1-terms",
   "text": "The bill is the closing costs: origination charges, discount points, the appraisal, title insurance, and the rest.",
   "start": 96.0,
   "end": 103.77
  },
  {
   "id": "a1-turn.1",
   "scene": "a1-turn",
   "text": "But that bill barely grows with the loan, so it lands much harder on some borrowers than on others.",
   "start": 104.22,
   "end": 110.59
  },
  {
   "id": "a1-dan.1",
   "scene": "a1-dan",
   "text": "Take Dan, who borrowed $115,000 in the same month, at the same rate.",
   "start": 111.04,
   "end": 117.73
  },
  {
   "id": "a1-dan2.1",
   "scene": "a1-dan2",
   "text": "On loans under $150,000, the median bill was 3.4% of the amount borrowed.",
   "start": 118.18,
   "end": 125.49
  },
  {
   "id": "a1-dan3.1",
   "scene": "a1-dan3",
   "text": "For Dan, that is $3,667: fewer dollars than Maya, but a far larger share of his loan.",
   "start": 125.94,
   "end": 135.83
  },
  {
   "id": "a1-priya.1",
   "scene": "a1-priya",
   "text": "Priya borrowed $1,005,000.",
   "start": 136.28,
   "end": 139.41
  },
  {
   "id": "a1-priya2.1",
   "scene": "a1-priya2",
   "text": "On loans of $750,000 and more, the bill was 0.5%.",
   "start": 139.86,
   "end": 145.42
  },
  {
   "id": "a1-priya3.1",
   "scene": "a1-priya3",
   "text": "For Priya, that is $5,034: about Maya's bill, on a loan nearly three times the size.",
   "start": 145.87,
   "end": 154.51
  },
  {
   "id": "a1-save.1",
   "scene": "a1-save",
   "text": "What the bill buys is a lower payment. At today's rate, Maya's payment falls by $221 a month.",
   "start": 154.96,
   "end": 163.1
  },
  {
   "id": "a1-rule.1",
   "scene": "a1-rule",
   "text": "Divide the bill by that saving, and you get the answer most calculators give: 24 months.",
   "start": 163.55,
   "end": 170.05
  },
  {
   "id": "a1-payoff.1",
   "scene": "a1-payoff",
   "text": "By that count, the refinance has paid for itself. Or has it?",
   "start": 170.5,
   "end": 175.84
  },
  {
   "id": "a2-q.1",
   "scene": "a2-q",
   "text": "Here is what that division leaves out.",
   "start": 177.74,
   "end": 180.42
  },
  {
   "id": "a2-old.1",
   "scene": "a2-old",
   "text": "Maya's old loan already has 35 payments behind it.",
   "start": 180.87,
   "end": 184.52
  },
  {
   "id": "a2-reset.1",
   "scene": "a2-reset",
   "text": "A refinance starts a fresh 30 years, so the new loan pays the balance down more slowly than the old one would have.",
   "start": 184.97,
   "end": 192.96
  },
  {
   "id": "a2-why.1",
   "scene": "a2-why",
   "text": "Early payments are mostly interest. Maya's old loan was just starting to pay down real principal, and the new one starts that schedule over.",
   "start": 193.41,
   "end": 202.58
  },
  {
   "id": "a2-owe.1",
   "scene": "a2-owe",
   "text": "So count what she still owes on each loan, month by month.",
   "start": 203.03,
   "end": 207.4
  },
  {
   "id": "a2-gap.1",
   "scene": "a2-gap",
   "text": "After 24 months, the new loan leaves her owing $1,133 more than the old one would have.",
   "start": 207.85,
   "end": 216.97
  },
  {
   "id": "a2-real.1",
   "scene": "a2-real",
   "text": "So, counting what she still owes, break-even is not 24 months. It is 30.",
   "start": 217.42,
   "end": 222.51
  },
  {
   "id": "a2-thesis.1",
   "scene": "a2-thesis",
   "text": "Most calculators stop at the monthly saving. The balance keeps the score for longer.",
   "start": 224.11,
   "end": 229.95
  },
  {
   "id": "a2-small.1",
   "scene": "a2-small",
   "text": "The smaller the cut, the bigger that gap.",
   "start": 230.4,
   "end": 233.58
  },
  {
   "id": "a2-s025.1",
   "scene": "a2-s025",
   "text": "At a cut of 0.25 points, the division would promise 38 months.",
   "start": 234.03,
   "end": 240.15
  },
  {
   "id": "a2-s025b.1",
   "scene": "a2-s025b",
   "text": "Counting the balance, Maya never catches up before her old loan would have been paid off.",
   "start": 240.6,
   "end": 246.84
  },
  {
   "id": "a2-cliff.1",
   "scene": "a2-cliff",
   "text": "Below 0.5 points, the fresh start can swallow the savings entirely.",
   "start": 247.29,
   "end": 252.93
  },
  {
   "id": "a2-s10.1",
   "scene": "a2-s10",
   "text": "At a cut of 1 point, the two answers nearly meet.",
   "start": 253.38,
   "end": 257.47
  },
  {
   "id": "a2-s10b.1",
   "scene": "a2-s10b",
   "text": "The division says 16 months; the balance says 18.",
   "start": 257.92,
   "end": 261.76
  },
  {
   "id": "a2-dan.1",
   "scene": "a2-dan",
   "text": "Now Dan. At today's rate, his payment falls by only $68 a month.",
   "start": 262.21,
   "end": 267.76
  },
  {
   "id": "a2-dan2.1",
   "scene": "a2-dan2",
   "text": "The division says 55 months. Counting what he still owes, it is 75.",
   "start": 268.21,
   "end": 274.4
  },
  {
   "id": "a2-dan3.1",
   "scene": "a2-dan3",
   "text": "If Dan sells after 3 years, the refinance leaves him $1,777 behind.",
   "start": 274.85,
   "end": 282.73
  },
  {
   "id": "a2-dan4.1",
   "scene": "a2-dan4",
   "text": "After 7 years, he is only $386 ahead.",
   "start": 283.18,
   "end": 288.16
  },
  {
   "id": "a2-priya.1",
   "scene": "a2-priya",
   "text": "Priya saves $593 a month, and is even after 11 months.",
   "start": 288.61,
   "end": 294.67
  },
  {
   "id": "a2-priya2.1",
   "scene": "a2-priya2",
   "text": "If she sells after 3 years, she is $11,482 ahead; after 7, $30,387.",
   "start": 295.12,
   "end": 306.6
  },
  {
   "id": "a2-maya.1",
   "scene": "a2-maya",
   "text": "And Maya, the middle case: $1,039 ahead after 3 years, $8,093 after 7.",
   "start": 307.05,
   "end": 315.35
  },
  {
   "id": "a2-sell.1",
   "scene": "a2-sell",
   "text": "Put another way: a house sold before the break-even month turns the refinance into a loss, and one kept past it turns it into a gain.",
   "start": 315.8,
   "end": 326.5
  },
  {
   "id": "a2-payoff.1",
   "scene": "a2-payoff",
   "text": "Same rate, same month. The answer depends on the size of the loan, how old it is, and how long the house is kept.",
   "start": 326.95,
   "end": 335.16
  },
  {
   "id": "a3-q.1",
   "scene": "a3-q",
   "text": "The division also assumes today's rate is the last one. What happened in the real drops?",
   "start": 337.06,
   "end": 343.43
  },
  {
   "id": "a3-all.1",
   "scene": "a3-all",
   "text": "We found every fall of at least 1 point in the 30-year rate since 1971: 13 of them.",
   "start": 343.88,
   "end": 350.72
  },
  {
   "id": "a3-rules.1",
   "scene": "a3-rules",
   "text": "In each, someone like Maya borrows at the peak and refinances in the first month the rate is 1 point lower.",
   "start": 351.17,
   "end": 358.99
  },
  {
   "id": "a3-costs.1",
   "scene": "a3-costs",
   "text": "Closing costs use each year's median share; before 2018, lenders did not report them, so a fixed share is used and marked illustrative.",
   "start": 359.44,
   "end": 369.55
  },
  {
   "id": "a3-range.1",
   "scene": "a3-range",
   "text": "Counting what was still owed, break-even took between 10 and 20 months.",
   "start": 370.0,
   "end": 374.86
  },
  {
   "id": "a3-simple.1",
   "scene": "a3-simple",
   "text": "The simple division would have said between 10 and 21.",
   "start": 375.31,
   "end": 378.99
  },
  {
   "id": "a3-young.1",
   "scene": "a3-young",
   "text": "Nearly the same, because those loans were only months old when the rate fell, so the fresh start cost them little. Maya's loan is 35 payments old.",
   "start": 379.44,
   "end": 389.58
  },
  {
   "id": "a3-81.1",
   "scene": "a3-81",
   "text": "The biggest fall began after the peak of 18.63% in 1981.",
   "start": 390.03,
   "end": 396.92
  },
  {
   "id": "a3-81b.1",
   "scene": "a3-81b",
   "text": "A refinance 2 months later paid back in 13 months, and the rate kept falling.",
   "start": 397.37,
   "end": 402.98
  },
  {
   "id": "a3-priya.1",
   "scene": "a3-priya",
   "text": "For Priya's loan size, every one of those drops paid back within 6 months.",
   "start": 403.43,
   "end": 410.37
  },
  {
   "id": "a3-dan.1",
   "scene": "a3-dan",
   "text": "For a loan like Dan's, with the bill a bigger share, the same drops took between 18 and 39 months.",
   "start": 410.82,
   "end": 419.21
  },
  {
   "id": "a3-turn.1",
   "scene": "a3-turn",
   "text": "But in 3 of those drops, the rate fell another 1 point before the first refinance had paid for itself.",
   "start": 419.66,
   "end": 427.26
  },
  {
   "id": "a3-twice.1",
   "scene": "a3-twice",
   "text": "Those borrowers met the same question twice, and a second refinance starts a new clock with a new bill.",
   "start": 427.71,
   "end": 435.07
  },
  {
   "id": "a3-example.1",
   "scene": "a3-example",
   "text": "In the drop that began in July 1984, the refinance came in November 1984.",
   "start": 435.52,
   "end": 442.56
  },
  {
   "id": "a3-example2.1",
   "scene": "a3-example2",
   "text": "It needed 19 months to pay back, and the next drop of 1 point came 7 months later.",
   "start": 443.01,
   "end": 450.05
  },
  {
   "id": "a3-quiet.1",
   "scene": "a3-quiet",
   "text": "In the other 10, the first refinance was the one that mattered.",
   "start": 450.5,
   "end": 455.15
  },
  {
   "id": "a3-23.1",
   "scene": "a3-23",
   "text": "Maya's own drop began in October 2023. A cut of 1 point first arrived in August 2024.",
   "start": 455.6,
   "end": 463.92
  },
  {
   "id": "a3-23be.1",
   "scene": "a3-23be",
   "text": "Refinancing then would have paid back in 19 months.",
   "start": 464.37,
   "end": 467.95
  },
  {
   "id": "a3-answer.1",
   "scene": "a3-answer",
   "text": "So, how far do rates have to fall before a refinance pays for itself?",
   "start": 468.4,
   "end": 473.36
  },
  {
   "id": "a3-answer2.1",
   "scene": "a3-answer2",
   "text": "For a loan like Maya's, counting what is still owed, a cut of 0.5 points pays it back within 36 months.",
   "start": 473.81,
   "end": 482.79
  },
  {
   "id": "a3-answer3.1",
   "scene": "a3-answer3",
   "text": "Dan would need 1.12 points. Priya, 0.2.",
   "start": 484.39,
   "end": 490.31
  },
  {
   "id": "a3-today.1",
   "scene": "a3-today",
   "text": "Today's cut is 0.59 points. For Maya, that means ahead after 30 months, and behind if the house is sold before then.",
   "start": 490.76,
   "end": 500.08
  },
  {
   "id": "a3-limit.1",
   "scene": "a3-limit",
   "text": "That answer has limits. It leaves out taxes and any return on the cash, and it assumes the bill is paid up front, not added to the loan.",
   "start": 501.28,
   "end": 511.07
  },
  {
   "id": "a3-median.1",
   "scene": "a3-median",
   "text": "It uses medians: half of borrowers paid more than $5,124, and an offered rate can differ from the survey average.",
   "start": 511.52,
   "end": 521.04
  },
  {
   "id": "a3-history.1",
   "scene": "a3-history",
   "text": "And it is history, not a forecast: nothing here says where rates go next.",
   "start": 521.49,
   "end": 527.03
  },
  {
   "id": "a3-close.1",
   "scene": "a3-close",
   "text": "What history does show is the shape of the trade: a known bill up front, against savings and a balance that settle month by month.",
   "start": 527.48,
   "end": 536.78
  },
  {
   "id": "m-rates.1",
   "scene": "m-rates",
   "text": "Method. Rates are Freddie Mac's weekly survey via FRED, checked against the Optimal Blue index since 2017.",
   "start": 537.23,
   "end": 544.98
  },
  {
   "id": "m-drops.1",
   "scene": "m-drops",
   "text": "A drop counts when the monthly average falls at least 1 point from a peak before rising 1 point from its low.",
   "start": 545.43,
   "end": 552.69
  },
  {
   "id": "m-costs.1",
   "scene": "m-costs",
   "text": "Costs are HMDA loan-level data from 2018 to 2025: originated refinances, first lien, 30-year term.",
   "start": 553.14,
   "end": 561.62
  },
  {
   "id": "m-chars.1",
   "scene": "m-chars",
   "text": "Maya, Dan and Priya are illustrative: each one's loan and bill are the medians for that loan size.",
   "start": 562.07,
   "end": 569.3
  },
  {
   "id": "m-assume.1",
   "scene": "m-assume",
   "text": "Break-even counts payment savings plus the difference in balance still owed. Closing costs paid in cash; no taxes. Every figure is listed in the description.",
   "start": 569.75,
   "end": 580.03
  },
  {
   "id": "o-recap.1",
   "scene": "o-recap",
   "text": "A small cut needs a long stay. A small loan needs a bigger cut. And a young loan loses less to the fresh start.",
   "start": 580.48,
   "end": 590.14
  },
  {
   "id": "o-you.1",
   "scene": "o-you",
   "text": "If the rate on your own statement starts with a 7, the same three questions apply: how big the cut, how old the loan, and how long the stay.",
   "start": 590.59,
   "end": 601.13
  },
  {
   "id": "o-close.1",
   "scene": "o-close",
   "text": "The monthly saving is only half of the answer. The other half, just as real, is what is still owed.",
   "start": 601.58,
   "end": 609.86
  },
  {
   "id": "o-sources.1",
   "scene": "o-sources",
   "text": "The sources are linked in the description, and the next decision is already on the bench.",
   "start": 610.31,
   "end": 616.67
  }
 ],
 "tailCut": {
  "co-cost.1": 3.38,
  "co-question.1": 3.36
 },
 "note": "PLANNED at M1 from the table-read takes; M2 re-times from the final takes"
